<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Analysis_1> a iadas:Analysis;
  iadas:analysisId "1";
  iadas:authorConclusion "This study highlights a positive association between exercise addiction and disordered eating, with body dysmorphic concerns and body appreciation acting as key mediating factors. It underscores the importance of psychological mechanisms in this relationship among young sport practitioners.";
  iadas:complexityOfAnalysis "Simple analyses";
  iadas:groupStatisticalAnalysis "Correlation";
  iadas:hasPopulation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Population_1>;
  iadas:hasRelation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Relations_1>;
  iadas:hasSport <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Range%20of%20sports>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/including%20endurance>,
    <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/strength-based>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/team%20sports>;
  iadas:limites "The cross-sectional design prevents causal inferences, and the use of self-reported measures may introduce bias (e.g., social desirability, inaccurate reporting). Additionally, the specific sample limits the generalizability of the findings.";
  iadas:numberOfSportStudied "Multiple";
  iadas:perspectives "Future longitudinal and cross-cultural studies are needed to better understand these relationships and underlying mechanisms. Interventions targeting body image may help prevent disordered eating behaviors in this population.";
  iadas:relationDegree "r=-.65, p<.001";
  iadas:relationDirection "-";
  iadas:sampleSizeMobilized "321";
  iadas:sportPracticeType "Mixed sport";
  iadas:sportSubcategory "Multisport";
  iadas:typeOfAnalysis "Pearson correlation" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Analysis_10> a iadas:Analysis;
  iadas:analysisId "10";
  iadas:authorConclusion "This study supports a dual-pathway model of muscle dysmorphia, where sociocultural pressure influences symptoms both indirectly via appearance motivation and directly, with cognitive flexibility acting as a protective moderator. It highlights the interplay between social, motivational, and cognitive factors";
  iadas:complexityOfAnalysis "Simple analyses";
  iadas:groupStatisticalAnalysis "Correlation";
  iadas:hasPopulation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Population_10>;
  iadas:hasRelation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Relations_10>;
  iadas:limites "The cross-sectional design limits causal interpretation, and the use of a convenience, all-male sample restricts generalizability. Additionally, the measure of sociocultural pressure was not specific to muscularity ideals";
  iadas:numberOfSportStudied "Multiple";
  iadas:perspectives "Future research should use longitudinal designs and more diverse samples to validate the model. Interventions enhancing cognitive flexibility and promoting non-appearance-based exercise motives appear promising avenues for prevention and treatment.";
  iadas:relationDegree "r=.605, p<.001";
  iadas:relationDirection "+";
  iadas:sampleSizeMobilized "95";
  iadas:sportPracticeType "Mixed sport";
  iadas:sportSubcategory "Multisport";
  iadas:typeOfAnalysis "Pearson correlation" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Analysis_100> a iadas:Analysis;
  iadas:analysisId "100";
  iadas:authorConclusion "The current findings support body talk and fat talk as a potential risk factor for eating disorders among male. Muscle-focused body talk may not a risk factor for diagnostic eating disorder symptoms. Muscle talk among male is related less to muscle dissatisfaction and more to male socioalization processes. Athletic identity did non emerge as a significant moderator of the association between body talk and eating disorder symptoms. ";
  iadas:complexityOfAnalysis "Simple analyses";
  iadas:groupStatisticalAnalysis "Correlation";
  iadas:hasPopulation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Population_100>;
  iadas:hasRelation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Relations_100>;
  iadas:hasSport <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Mixed%20sport>;
  iadas:limites "The study was limited to a single university campus in the southeastern United States, limiting generalizability of the findings. Few participants endorsed aesthetic or lean sports, potentially attenuating results. ";
  iadas:numberOfSportStudied "Multiple";
  iadas:perspectives """Future work may benefit from using a more objective of body composition. 
Future work should explore such associations among male in a clinical sample, examining whether fat or muscle talk is associated with eating disorder and/or muscle dysmorphia symptom severity. 
Fat talk may be an important prevention target amon male, regardless of athletic identity. """;
  iadas:relationDegree "r=.63, p<.001";
