<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Analysis_1009> a iadas:Analysis;
  iadas:analysisId "1009";
  iadas:authorConclusion "Bodybuilding may represent a practice that encourages male to become more self-conscious of their bodies, putting them at greater risk of experiencing greater levels of monitoring of body appearance and anxiety about physical appearance. This body self consciousness is associated with a greater risk of body dissatisfaction and drive for muscularity. Weightlifting does not appear to represent an objectifying climate. ";
  iadas:complexityOfAnalysis "Simple analyses";
  iadas:groupStatisticalAnalysis "Correlation";
  iadas:hasPopulation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Population_1009>;
  iadas:hasRelation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Relations_1009>;
  iadas:hasSport <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Bodybuilding>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Soccer>,
    <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Weightlifting>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/football>,
    <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/martial%20arts>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/running>,
    <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/swimming>;
  iadas:limites "The cross sectional nature makes it possible only to speculate on the directions of causality. Unequal sample sizes of groups in our analyses, particularly with respect to fewer competitive weightlifters. Unknown selection bias that may be operating in the bodybuilder and weightlifter groups, and any subsequent influence on the interpretation of our results. The weighlifting and bodybuilder groups differed not only in the nature of their sport, but also in competitive nature of this sport, which may introduce confound for differences found between the groups. ";
  iadas:numberOfSportStudied "Multiple";
  iadas:perspectives "Health pursuits for male should emphasize achievement and function rather than appearance if they are to avoid deleterious consesquences. ";
  iadas:relationDegree "r=.09, N.S.";
  iadas:relationDirection "NS";
  iadas:sampleSizeMobilized "83";
  iadas:sportPracticeType "Mixed sport";
  iadas:sportSubcategory "Multisport ";
  iadas:typeOfAnalysis "Correlation" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Analysis_101> a iadas:Analysis;
  iadas:analysisId "101";
  iadas:authorConclusion "The current findings support body talk and fat talk as a potential risk factor for eating disorders among male. Muscle-focused body talk may not a risk factor for diagnostic eating disorder symptoms. Muscle talk among male is related less to muscle dissatisfaction and more to male socioalization processes. Athletic identity did non emerge as a significant moderator of the association between body talk and eating disorder symptoms. ";
  iadas:complexityOfAnalysis "Simple analyses";
  iadas:groupStatisticalAnalysis "Correlation";
  iadas:hasPopulation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Population_101>;
  iadas:hasRelation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Relations_101>;
  iadas:hasSport <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Mixed%20sport>;
  iadas:limites "The study was limited to a single university campus in the southeastern United States, limiting generalizability of the findings. Few participants endorsed aesthetic or lean sports, potentially attenuating results. ";
  iadas:numberOfSportStudied "Multiple";
  iadas:perspectives """Future work may benefit from using a more objective of body composition. 
Future work should explore such associations among male in a clinical sample, examining whether fat or muscle talk is associated with eating disorder and/or muscle dysmorphia symptom severity. 
Fat talk may be an important prevention target amon male, regardless of athletic identity. """;
  iadas:relationDegree "r=.27, p<.001";
  iadas:relationDirection "+";
  iadas:sampleSizeMobilized "224";
  iadas:sportPracticeType "Mixed sport";
  iadas:sportSubcategory "Multisport ";
  iadas:typeOfAnalysis "Correlation" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Analysis_1010> a iadas:Analysis;
  iadas:analysisId "1010";
  iadas:authorConclusion "Bodybuilding may represent a practice that encourages male to become more self-conscious of their bodies, putting them at greater risk of experiencing greater levels of monitoring of body appearance and anxiety about physical appearance. This body self consciousness is associated with a greater risk of body dissatisfaction and drive for muscularity. Weightlifting does not appear to represent an objectifying climate. ";
  iadas:complexityOfAnalysis "Simple analyses";
  iadas:groupStatisticalAnalysis "Correlation";
  iadas:hasPopulation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Population_1010>;
  iadas:hasRelation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Relations_1010>;
  iadas:hasSport <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Bodybuilding>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Soccer>,
    <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Weightlifting>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/football>,
    <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/martial%20arts>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/running>,
    <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/swimming>;
  iadas:limites "The cross sectional nature makes it possible only to speculate on the directions of causality. Unequal sample sizes of groups in our analyses, particularly with respect to fewer competitive weightlifters. Unknown selection bias that may be operating in the bodybuilder and weightlifter groups, and any subsequent influence on the interpretation of our results. The weighlifting and bodybuilder groups differed not only in the nature of their sport, but also in competitive nature of this sport, which may introduce confound for differences found between the groups. ";
  iadas:numberOfSportStudied "Multiple";
  iadas:perspectives "Health pursuits for male should emphasize achievement and function rather than appearance if they are to avoid deleterious consesquences. ";
  iadas:relationDegree "r=.05, N.S.";
