<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Analysis_1062> a iadas:Analysis;
  iadas:analysisId "1062";
  iadas:authorConclusion "Ego orientation and performance climate were related to more frequent dieting, using more pathogenic weight control methods, greater perfectionism and perceiving more weight related peer pressure. Having a high ego orientation means that a Female aesthetic performer is sgnificantly more likely to report indices of disordered eating. Non-significant correlations were found between task orientation and DE. With respect to performance climate, significant relationships were found with all DE variables. The present study also shows that a mastery climate is negatively related to disordered eating. Mastery climate explained some of the variance of dieting frequency. ";
  iadas:complexityOfAnalysis "Simple analyses";
  iadas:groupStatisticalAnalysis "Correlation";
  iadas:hasPopulation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Population_1062>;
  iadas:hasRelation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Relations_1062>;
  iadas:hasSport <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Dance>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/gymnastics>;
  iadas:limites "A limitation in the present study was that we used a relatively crude measure for pathogenic weight control with limited reliability. Another limitation was that we had to confine ourselves to self-reported height and weight because objective measurement of body composition was perceived as too threatening form some aesthetic performers. Self-report holds the risk of both under or overstimation. Another constraint in the present study was the composition of our sample. As the number of high level competitive gymnasts in the Netherlands is limited, we also investigated a group of high level Female dancers. ";
  iadas:numberOfSportStudied "Multiple";
  iadas:perspectives "In future studes focusing exclusively on relationships between eating disorders or disordered eating with achievement motivation, full versions of general or sport specific measures could be administered such as the Eating Disorder Inventory-2 or the Female Athlete Screening Tool for disordered eating. Future studies should continue to making an effort to include objective measurements by the researchers or recent actual data provided by medical staff members. The observed relationships of this study should be replicated in future studies using larger samples of various athletes at risk for disordered eating. Roberts (2001) stated that a high ego orientation is particularly detrimental if it is accompanied by a low task orientation. Future research should further investigate if this is also true for disordered eating. To gain a more complete understanding of the relationship between ego orientation and disordered eating, future studies could also include the more recent 2 by 2 framework which not only distinguishes between ego and task orientation but also between approach and avoidance achievmalet goals. We therefore recommaled that coaches and teachers should create a mastery climate with its emphasis on personal development and interpersonal cooperation. ";
  iadas:relationDegree "r=.33, p<.05";
  iadas:relationDirection "+";
  iadas:sampleSizeMobilized "94";
  iadas:sportPracticeType "Individual sport";
  iadas:sportSubcategory "Aesthetic";
  iadas:typeOfAnalysis "Correlation" .

