<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Analysis_107> a iadas:Analysis;
  iadas:analysisId "107";
  iadas:authorConclusion "The current findings support body talk and fat talk as a potential risk factor for eating disorders among male. Muscle-focused body talk may not a risk factor for diagnostic eating disorder symptoms. Muscle talk among male is related less to muscle dissatisfaction and more to male socioalization processes. Athletic identity did non emerge as a significant moderator of the association between body talk and eating disorder symptoms. ";
  iadas:complexityOfAnalysis "Simple analyses";
  iadas:groupStatisticalAnalysis "Correlation";
  iadas:hasPopulation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Population_107>;
  iadas:hasRelation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Relations_107>;
  iadas:hasSport <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Mixed%20sport>;
  iadas:limites "The study was limited to a single university campus in the southeastern United States, limiting generalizability of the findings. Few participants endorsed aesthetic or lean sports, potentially attenuating results. ";
  iadas:numberOfSportStudied "Multiple";
  iadas:perspectives """Future work may benefit from using a more objective of body composition. 
Future work should explore such associations among male in a clinical sample, examining whether fat or muscle talk is associated with eating disorder and/or muscle dysmorphia symptom severity. 
Fat talk may be an important prevention target amon male, regardless of athletic identity. """;
  iadas:relationDegree "r=.41 , p<.01";
  iadas:relationDirection "+";
  iadas:sampleSizeMobilized "224";
  iadas:sportPracticeType "Mixed sport";
  iadas:sportSubcategory "Multisport ";
  iadas:typeOfAnalysis "Correlation" .

