<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Analysis_112> a iadas:Analysis;
  iadas:analysisId "112";
  iadas:authorConclusion "This study highlights a higher risk of orthorexia nervosa (OrNe) among male students, with strong associations with dieting behaviors and, in females, BMI. It emphasizes the multifactorial nature of OrNe and the importance of early identification in young athletes.";
  iadas:complexityOfAnalysis "Simple analyses";
  iadas:groupStatisticalAnalysis "Correlation";
  iadas:hasPopulation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Population_112>;
  iadas:hasRelation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Relations_112>;
  iadas:hasSport <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Badminton>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/basketball>,
    <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/football>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/other%20sport>,
    <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/swimming>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/volleyball>,
    <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/weightlifting>;
  iadas:limites "The cross-sectional design limits causal inference, and self-reported measures may introduce bias. Additionally, mental health assessment tools and limited comparability with prior studies restrict interpretation.";
  iadas:numberOfSportStudied "Multiple";
  iadas:perspectives "Future research should include longitudinal designs, more diverse populations, and more specific mental health measures. Preventive strategies targeting dietary behaviors and body-related factors in university and sports settings are needed.";
  iadas:relationDegree "r=-.007, p=.903";
  iadas:relationDirection "NS";
  iadas:sampleSizeMobilized "272";
  iadas:sportPracticeType "Mixed sport";
  iadas:sportSubcategory "Multisport";
  iadas:typeOfAnalysis "Pearson correlation" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Analysis_1120> a iadas:Analysis;
  iadas:analysisId "1120";
  iadas:authorConclusion "Variables such as sex, age, BMI and body dissatisfaction explained 52% of the variance. Body dissatisfaction was the strongest predictor. Sports related variables were able to account for an additional 9% of variance in disorded eating. The most influential was the desire to be leaner to improve sports performance. General body dissatisfaction, sports-related body dissatisfaction was not a significant predictor for disordered eating. General and sports-related body dissatisfaction shared 37% of variance. ";
  iadas:complexityOfAnalysis "Simple analyses";
  iadas:groupStatisticalAnalysis "Regression";
  iadas:hasPopulation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Population_1120>;
  iadas:hasRelation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Relations_1120>;
  iadas:hasSport <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Ice%20figure%20skating>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/ballet>,
    <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/basketball>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/diving>,
    <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/golf>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/gymnastics>,
    <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/judo>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/karate>,
    <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/roller-skate%20figure%20skating>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/running>,
    <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/rythmic%20gymnastics>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/sailing>,
    <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/soccer>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/swimming>;
  iadas:limites "Our sample size was too small to test for differences if gender was included in the analyzes as well. The EAT-26 was developed with non-athletic samples. The assessment of body dissatisfaction needs to be discussed critically. We opted for measures that focus on body fat and left out the dimension of muscularity. Nonetheless, muscularity might be important as to how athletes perceive their ideal body and more valid especially in male athletes. For ATHLETES questionnaires, limitations result from adapting and applying the questuonnaire within our sample. ";
  iadas:numberOfSportStudied "Multiple";
  iadas:perspectives """Future studies could apply more differentiated assessments of disordered eating which allow analyzing if risk factors are the same for restricted and bulimic eating patterns. 
Achievement goal theory appears very promising for further studies that seek to understand the different risks for eating disorders in athletes. Longitudinal and experimental investigations are necessary to test whether the sports-related correlates of disordered eating tested in our study constitute risk factors for disordered eating and whether the assumed causal direction can be confirmed. A comparison between sport types regarding risk factors could help to explain why the risk for disordered eating is higher in certain sport types. """;
  iadas:relationDegree """𝛃=.49, p=.00
ΔR²=.43 - R²=.52
""";
  iadas:relationDirection "+";
  iadas:sampleSizeMobilized "96";
  iadas:sportPracticeType "Mixed sport";
  iadas:sportSubcategory "Multisport ";
  iadas:typeOfAnalysis "Simple linear regression" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Analysis_1125> a iadas:Analysis;
  iadas:analysisId "1125";
  iadas:authorConclusion "The desire to be leaner to improve sports performance was predictive of DE and not vice versa. Emotional distress resulting from missed exercise sessions and social pressure from the sports environment had no significant impact on DE. Sports-related body disssatisfaction is not predictive of DE when other sports-related variables are also considered. ";
  iadas:complexityOfAnalysis "Simple analyses";
  iadas:groupStatisticalAnalysis "Correlation";
  iadas:hasPopulation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Population_1125>;
  iadas:hasRelation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Relations_1125>;
  iadas:hasSport <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/ballet>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/diving>,
    <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/gymnastics>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/ice%20figure%20skating>,
    <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/rhythmic%20gymnastics>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/roller-skate%20figure%20skating>;
  iadas:limites "The rather short follow-up period of 1 year. Owing to our rather small sample size, the generalization of our results is further limited. Our study focuses on only one high-risk group. High performance athletes might not report pathological eating patterns because they fear being excluded from their team sports. Inclusion of only four potential risk factors";
  iadas:numberOfSportStudied "Multiple";
  iadas:perspectives "It would be interesting to investigate whther the desire to be leaner to improve sports performance is a risk factor for disordered eating in other types and how this desire is related to other (also non-sports-related) risk factors. More studies with a longitudinal design are needed to deepen our knowledge of the developmental course of disordered eating in athletes. ";
  iadas:relationDegree "r=.69, p<.01";
  iadas:relationDirection "+";
  iadas:sampleSizeMobilized "65";
  iadas:sportPracticeType "Individual sport";
  iadas:sportSubcategory "Aesthetic";
  iadas:typeOfAnalysis "Cross-lagged partial correlation" .

