  iadas:perspectives "Future research could usefully further investigate the perceived autonomy of dancers in relation to their passion, and the role that teachers play in the development of the two types of passion in the studio environment. ";
  iadas:relationDegree "r=-.02, p=N.S.";
  iadas:relationDirection "NS";
  iadas:sampleSizeMobilized "92";
  iadas:sportPracticeType "Individual sport";
  iadas:sportSubcategory "Aesthetic";
  iadas:typeOfAnalysis "Correlation" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Analysis_1548> a iadas:Analysis;
  iadas:analysisId "1548";
  iadas:authorConclusion "Body image related cognitive fusion was strongly associated with ED. A strong relationship was found between image related perfectionist self presentation and higher levels of disordered eating. Path analysis results demonstraed that the proposed model presents an excellent fit to the empirical data, accounting for 71% of the variance of disordered eating attitudes and behaviours. ";
  iadas:complexityOfAnalysis "Simple analyses";
  iadas:groupStatisticalAnalysis "Correlation";
  iadas:hasPopulation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Population_1548>;
  iadas:hasRelation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Relations_1548>;
  iadas:hasSport <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/dance>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/gymnastics>,
    <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/skating>;
  iadas:limites "Cross sectional study limits any causal inferences. This study only focused on Female participants. Use of data exclusively based on self report measures. Considering that disordered eating is a multi determined phenomaleon, other emotional processes should be considered to explain this phenomaleon. ";
  iadas:numberOfSportStudied "Multiple";
  iadas:perspectives """Future prospective studies should be conducted to clarify the associations between these variables and propose causal inferences. Also future research is needed to compare the acceptability and impact of cognitive behavioural therapy and acceptance and commitment therapy based interventions to promote athletes adaptive ways to deal with their body image. 
Data highlights the relevance of education programmes that promote more adaptive emotional strategies focusing on body image, to increase athletes defusion skills and the acceptance of unpleasant thoughts and emotions related to their body image. Our data seems to indicate that intervention and prevention programs for aesthetic athletes, should focus on the development of more adpative strategies related to body image, through the cultivation of a balanced and acceptance relationship with one's own body characteristics rather than on the adoption of body image related perfectionist self presentation strategies. """;
  iadas:relationDegree "r=.80, p<.001";
  iadas:relationDirection "+";
  iadas:sampleSizeMobilized "142";
  iadas:sportPracticeType "Individual sport";
  iadas:sportSubcategory "Aesthetic";
  iadas:typeOfAnalysis "Correlation" .

