  iadas:typeOfAnalysis "Correlation" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Analysis_1616> a iadas:Analysis;
  iadas:analysisId "1616";
  iadas:authorConclusion "Weight pressures from parents, peers and the media were directly associated with body dissatisfaction; moreover these pressures were indirectly associated with disordered eating via their associations with thin and muscular ideal internalization. In the full model, weight pressures specific to the sport context were not associated with body dissatisfaction or disordered eating. The degree to which athletes internalized ideals for thinness and muscularity was associated with higher levels of disordered eating. Body dissatisfaction was not associated with disordered eating after other variables in the model were taken into account. ";
  iadas:complexityOfAnalysis "Simple analyses";
  iadas:groupStatisticalAnalysis "Correlation";
  iadas:hasPopulation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Population_1616>;
  iadas:hasRelation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Relations_1616>;
  iadas:hasSport <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Basketball>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/cross-country>,
    <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/diving>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/gymnastics>,
    <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/lacrosse>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/soccer>,
    <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/swimming>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/track%20and%20field>;
  iadas:limites "A comprehensive understanding of sociocultural influences on disordered eating among athletes require not only greater representation of participants from marginazlied racial and ethnic groups, but also consideration of the role of intersecting marginalized identities (e.g., sexual orientation) and integration of qualitative and mixed methodological approaches that may more effectively characterizz the lived experiences of these athletes. Cross sectional design ; it is not possible to determine whether these characteristics are risk factors for disordered eating or correlates of these behaviors. ";
  iadas:numberOfSportStudied "Multiple";
  iadas:perspectives """There is a clear need for high-quality studies of athletes assessing weight related pressures, appearance ideal internalization, body satisfaction, and disordered eating over time. in addition to clarifying the temporal relationships among these characteristics, longitudinal designs could also address whether these relationships are stable at different stages of athletes careers or different time points throughout the athletic season. Future research sould also consider how sociocultural influences might contribute to positive eating-related outcomes, both in the sport context and more broadly. 
Interventions that specifically address the sociocultural pressures and appearance ideals most relevant to athletes, such as the Female Athlete Body Project, reduce risk for subsequent disordered eating behaviors. 
In addition to pressures and appearance ideals specific to the sports environment, eating disorder prevention programs for Female athletes should address general sociocultural pressures related to weight and appearance. These interventions should also address internalization of cultural ideals for appearance beyond thinness; for example dissonance-based interventions could challenge internalization of fit and muscular ideals and sport specific ideals for weight and shape. Interventions could also be enhanced by strengths-based approaches that build on elements of sport participation that appear to be protective, such as the contribution of athletes positive evaluations of their physical abilities and condition to body esteem. """;
  iadas:relationDegree "r=.50, p<.001";
  iadas:relationDirection "+";
  iadas:sampleSizeMobilized "212";
  iadas:sportPracticeType "Mixed sport";
  iadas:sportSubcategory "Multisport ";
  iadas:typeOfAnalysis "Correlation" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Analysis_1618> a iadas:Analysis;
  iadas:analysisId "1618";
  iadas:authorConclusion "BDDR and EA are positively but weakly associated, indicating they may co-occur but represent distinct and complex phenomena that cannot be explained by one another alone";
  iadas:complexityOfAnalysis "Simple analyses";
  iadas:groupStatisticalAnalysis "Correlation";
  iadas:hasPopulation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Population_1618>;
  iadas:hasRelation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Relations_1618>;
  iadas:hasSport <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Fitness>;
  iadas:limites "The study is limited by a restricted gym sample, time constraints, and potential participation bias due to the sensitivity of BDDR and male-dominated settings. These factors may have affected sample balance and generalizability";
  iadas:numberOfSportStudied "One";
  iadas:perspectives "Future research should explore tailored interventions addressing both BDDR and EA, particularly across different age groups and contexts. There is also a need for broader, more diverse samples and integrated mental health screening in fitness settings";
  iadas:relationDegree "r=.1519, p<.05";
  iadas:relationDirection "+";
  iadas:sampleSizeMobilized "179";
  iadas:sportPracticeType "Individual sport";
  iadas:sportSubcategory "Aesthetic";
  iadas:typeOfAnalysis "Pearson correlation" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Analysis_1619> a iadas:Analysis;
  iadas:analysisId "1619";
  iadas:authorConclusion "The relation between perfectionism and eating disorders symptoms was partially mediated by thinness related learning. The hypothesis that the relationship between thinness related learning and eating disorders symptoms were partially mediated by thiness restricting expectancies was also supported. A direct relationship between perfectionism and eating disorders symptoms was found. ";
  iadas:complexityOfAnalysis "Simple analyses";
  iadas:groupStatisticalAnalysis "Correlation";
  iadas:hasPopulation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Population_1619>;
  iadas:hasRelation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Relations_1619>;
  iadas:hasSport <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Dance%20%28classical%29>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Dance%20%28jazz%29>;
  iadas:limites "The data was cross sectional. Although the current model fit the data well, there are multiple other risk factors for ED and it is very likely that this risk is determined by multiple factors and is not simply a function of the emphasis on thinness in the dance environment, or perfectionism. The sample was too small to split into professional and non professional dancers to examine if the model was the same in these groups. Our view of perfectionism was aligned with the construct of clinical perfectionism, and as a result we examined a selected subset of scales from the FMPS and did not use relevant scales from the HMPS. ";
  iadas:numberOfSportStudied "One";
  iadas:perspectives """It would be useful for future research to examine the efficacy of targeting young Female involved in dance with elevated levels of perfectionism for prevention programs, as preliminary findings are positive for the ability of programs targeting perfectionism to reduce eating disorders risk in Female adolescents. 
It would be useful for clinicians to consider directly targeting perfectionism through CBT programs for perfectionism in young Female involved in dance classes as it is likely to be a factor that puts them at higher risk of developing an eating disorders within this environment. """;
  iadas:relationDegree " r=.74, p<.001";
  iadas:relationDirection "+";
  iadas:sampleSizeMobilized "142";
  iadas:sportPracticeType "Individual sport";
  iadas:sportSubcategory "Aesthetic";
  iadas:typeOfAnalysis "Correlation" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Analysis_162> a iadas:Analysis;
  iadas:analysisId "162";
  iadas:authorConclusion "Sport pressures and dietary restraint were highly stable over the course of a competitive season. Pressures gymnasts and swimmers/divers experienced regarding body weight, size, and appearance from their coaches and teammates predicted decreases in their body satisfaction over a 5-month period; this change occurred even though the athletes’ body mass index did not change significantly over that time.";
  iadas:complexityOfAnalysis "Simple analyses";
  iadas:groupStatisticalAnalysis "Correlation";
  iadas:hasPopulation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Population_162>;
  iadas:hasRelation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Relations_162>;
  iadas:hasSport <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/divers>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/gymnastics>,
    <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/swimming>;
  iadas:limites "Included only NCAA Division I Female gymnasts and swimmers.divers, who also happened to primarily be Caucasien. The authors examined the variables over only a 5-month period, which represented one competitive collegiate season for these athletes. They examined the relationships among only sport pressures, body dissatisfaction and dietary restraint, additional research is needed to examine other variables such as general sociatail pressures, internalization, and negative affect. The validity of self-report measures of dietary restraint as predictors of actual reductions in food intake has been challenged. Self report measures of dietary restraint are risk factors in the prediction of disordered eating attitudes and behaviors, the measures are poor predictors of behaviorally observed, acute dietary restriction. BMI did not vary over the course of the 5-month season, we were not able to obtain more sensitive measures of body composition due to the study's design. ";
  iadas:numberOfSportStudied "Multiple";
  iadas:perspectives """Health care personnel can help coaches and teammates become more aware of how, through their behaviors and comments, they create an environment that pressures athletes about their body, weight, and appearance. Sport health care professionals can target sport administrators and sport-governing bodies because they have the power to male policy changes that can improve the sport environment, maling it less toxic for athletes. Such entities, could make revealing uniforms less so, adjust judging criteria so there is less focus on aesthetic/appearance-based aspects of the performance, expand or raise weight classifications, and/or set up strict requirements regarding acceptable weight loss over specific periods of time. Preventive programming, sport health care profesionals can work directly with athletes to help them counter these sport pressures and reduce their likelihood of engaging in caloric restriction of feeling dissatisfied with their bodies. 
Future reseach should examine whether our findings are supported in other groups of Female athletes (e.g., different sports, different competitive levels, more diverse racial/ethnic groups); male athletes should also be studied and such research should include measures of drive for muscularity. Other longitudinal studies may want to examine eating disorders and other potential risk factors over a longer period of time, or a different period (e.g., from the end of the competitive season to the start of the next one). """;
  iadas:relationDegree " r=.68, p<.0001";
  iadas:relationDirection "+";
  iadas:sampleSizeMobilized "325";
  iadas:sportPracticeType "Mixed sport";
  iadas:sportSubcategory "Multisport ";
  iadas:typeOfAnalysis "Correlation" .

