  iadas:hasRelation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Relations_1721>;
  iadas:hasSport <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Football>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/alpine%20skiing>,
    <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/basketball>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/cheerleading>,
    <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/cross-country>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/cross-country%20skiing>,
    <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/diving>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/fencing>,
    <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/golf>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/gymnastics>,
    <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/ice%20hockey>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/lacrosse>,
    <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/swimming>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/tennis>,
    <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/track%20and%20field>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/volleyball>;
  iadas:limites "Negative mood was represented by a single-item measures, this may have affected the relationship with disordered eating. All data are based on athlete self report and thus are subject to mono-method bias as well as underreporting that might occur when assessing sensitive areas. Only two of the male athletes were classified as having an eating disorder and both had an ED-NOS. ";
  iadas:numberOfSportStudied "Multiple";
  iadas:perspectives "Researchers should use measures of specific mood states (e.g., depression, anxiety) that are based on multiple items (e.g., PANAS-X), include measures of positive mood state (e.g., optimism, happiness, confidence) and incorporate designs (e.g., longitudinal) that allow these questions to be addressed directly. Professionals should be attuned to looking for subclinical level presentations of symptoms among the athletes with whom they work. Professionals may want to work proactively with male athletic teams, educating them about healthy, nutritional eating and maintaing regular exercise during the off-season. Such approaches may assist male collegiate athletes during the shift from the structural regimen that occurs when in-season and the potentially more relaxed, self-initiated approach that is taken during the off season. ";
  iadas:relationDegree " r=.44, p<.001";
  iadas:relationDirection "+";
  iadas:sampleSizeMobilized "35";
  iadas:sportPracticeType "Mixed sport";
  iadas:sportSubcategory "Multisport ";
  iadas:typeOfAnalysis "Correlation" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Analysis_1722> a iadas:Analysis;
  iadas:analysisId "1722";
  iadas:authorConclusion "The pressures participants felt from general media (e.g., magazines, television/movies) rather than from sport-specific (e.g., teammates, judges) and interpersonal (e.g., boy/girlfriend, friends) sources most strongly discriminated among the three eating disorder categories. The correlations between internalization and the discriminant functin were significant but small. Athlete, relative to nonathletes, may be more satisfied with their bodies, this variable is related strongly to eating pathology. Body image, internalization, negative affect and pressures contributed similarly to the differentiation among the three eating disorder categories. ";
  iadas:complexityOfAnalysis "Simple analyses";
  iadas:groupStatisticalAnalysis "Correlation";
  iadas:hasPopulation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Population_1722>;
  iadas:hasRelation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Relations_1722>;
  iadas:hasSport <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Basketball>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/alpine%20skiing>,
    <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/cheerleading>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/cross-country>,
    <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/cross-country%20skiing>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/diving>,
    <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/fencing>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/field%20hockey>,
    <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/golf>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/gymnastics>,
    <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/ice%20hockey>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/lacrosse>,
    <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/rowing>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/soccer>,
    <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/softball>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/sport%20shooting>,
    <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/swimming>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/synchronized%20swimming>,
    <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/tennis>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/track%20and%20field>,
    <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/volleyball>;
  iadas:limites "All data were self reported, which allowed for the possibility of socially desirable responding. The cross sectional design did not allow for determining temporal relationships among the variables and eating pathology. ";
  iadas:numberOfSportStudied "Multiple";
  iadas:perspectives """Future sudies may want to employ Petrie and Greenleaf's (2007) conceptual model using structural equation modeling and incorporate qualitative interviews to better determine how Female athletes experience and cope with the pressure from coaches, and whether these pressures change over the time they are involved in their sports. Future research should include measure of dietary restraint to determine if it assists in accurately classifying athletes into the three eating disorder groups. 
Professionals need to develop methods for screening athletes prior to each season to identify eating disorder and symptomatic groups and intervene as early as possible. Body image groups that focus on decreasing internalization and the effects of societal pressures may be an effective intervention strategy with Female athletes. """;
  iadas:relationDegree "r=.25, p=.02";
  iadas:relationDirection "+";
  iadas:sampleSizeMobilized "397";
  iadas:sportPracticeType "Mixed sport";
  iadas:sportSubcategory "Multisport ";
  iadas:typeOfAnalysis "Correlation" .

