<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Analysis_1822> a iadas:Analysis;
  iadas:analysisId "1822";
  iadas:authorConclusion "Results support the importance of teammate body comparison, pressure, and relationships for muscularity oriented disordered eating and weight and shape concerns amongst collegiate athletes. ";
  iadas:complexityOfAnalysis "Simple analyses";
  iadas:groupStatisticalAnalysis "Correlation";
  iadas:hasPopulation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Population_1822>;
  iadas:hasRelation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Relations_1822>;
  iadas:limites "The study had varying rates of participation across varsity and club athlete groups that led to unequal representation in the final sample and inhibited group comparison. The study small sample size. The data were self-reported, cross-sectional and collecte from athletes from only one division I university. ";
  iadas:numberOfSportStudied "Multiple";
  iadas:perspectives "Interventions such as educational programming amongst teams, compaings including on social media to promote positive repesentation of embodiment among athletes, as well as access to appropriate mental health resources are needed to protect athletes from disordered eating habits and negative body image comparisons. Future studies could utilize the model created to further explore the nunances of athlete-teammate relationships in larger, more diverse athlete populations and sporting environments. ";
  iadas:relationDegree "r=.59, p<.01";
  iadas:relationDirection "+";
  iadas:sampleSizeMobilized "156";
  iadas:sportPracticeType "Mixed sport";
  iadas:sportSubcategory "Multisport ";
  iadas:typeOfAnalysis "Intercorrelation" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Analysis_1824> a iadas:Analysis;
  iadas:analysisId "1824";
  iadas:authorConclusion "Drive for muscularity was positively associated was associated with binge eating and restricting. While we did find an association between body satisfaction and restricting eating behavior, there was not a significant association among binge eating, purging, and body satisfaction. Coaches/teammates were only a significant predictor of restricting behavior and were the least important predictor of it compared to general pressures and sport appearance pressures. ";
  iadas:complexityOfAnalysis "Simple analyses";
  iadas:groupStatisticalAnalysis "Correlation";
  iadas:hasPopulation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Population_1824>;
  iadas:hasRelation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Relations_1824>;
  iadas:hasSport <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Wrestling>;
  iadas:limites "The majority of participants identiied as white ; the present study sample is not representative of the racial makeup of this population. There was a relatively low response rate to the survey, which limits the generalizability of our sample. This study was cross sectional and only examined athletes in season, which limits the conclusions we can draw. The use of self report measures means it is possible that participants downplayed some of their behaviors or were unaware of the frequency of them. In addition, we did not ask specifically about frequency of binge eating. ";
  iadas:numberOfSportStudied "One";
  iadas:perspectives """One possible intervention is by providing coaches and athletes a decision tree to plan a safe weight-cutting strategu such as consuming strategic level of carbohydrates and fluids. It is important that wrestlers evaluate their weight-cutting and recovery practices before and after competitions to assess what is working safely. 
Future research should prioritize longitudinal studies that assess these variables in season versus out of season. Body satisfaction and disordered eating should be investigated in a racially diverse sample for both generalizability and differential risk. Future studies should examine Female's wrestling and explore gender differences in body satisfaction and disordered eaating behaviors. """;
  iadas:relationDegree "r=.41, p<.006";
  iadas:relationDirection "+";
  iadas:sampleSizeMobilized "103";
  iadas:sportPracticeType "Individual sport";
  iadas:sportSubcategory "Weight sensitive";
  iadas:typeOfAnalysis "Correlation" .

