<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Analysis_1868> a iadas:Analysis;
  iadas:analysisId "1868";
  iadas:authorConclusion "The significant negative associations between relationships with significant others and disordered eating attitudes which have been observed confirmed the protective role of social environment. No significant relationship was found between perceived physical appearances and disordered eating attitudes. Perceived physical ability was negatively related to disordered eating attitudes in the Canadian sample. In the French sample, these variable were positively related. ";
  iadas:complexityOfAnalysis "Simple analyses";
  iadas:groupStatisticalAnalysis "Correlation";
  iadas:hasPopulation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Population_1868>;
  iadas:hasRelation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Relations_1868>;
  iadas:hasSport <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Figure%20skating>;
  iadas:limites "The two samples were fairly homogenous. The results were based on only one small sample per country. ";
  iadas:numberOfSportStudied "One";
  iadas:perspectives """Further exploration of how eating disorders are manifested in various ethnic groups and the conditions under which elite athletes are at most risk may help to clarify where and when an unhealthy athletic environment is likely to exist and how these athletes may be assisted. 
Because culture is defined as shared motives, values, beliefs, identities, and interpretations or meanings of significant events that result from common experiences of members of collectives, it would be interesting to explore the role of these constructs and norms internalization and beliefs in the context of aesthetic sport on eating attitudes. """;
  iadas:relationDegree " r=.04, N.S.";
  iadas:relationDirection "NS";
  iadas:sampleSizeMobilized "120";
  iadas:sportPracticeType "Individual sport";
  iadas:sportSubcategory "Aesthetic";
  iadas:typeOfAnalysis "Pearson correlation" .

