<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Analysis_1965> a iadas:Analysis;
  iadas:analysisId "1965";
  iadas:authorConclusion "Critical comment severity was found to be more important than implicit teammate influeces when explaining increased drive for thinness, body dissatisfaction for Female athletes. Implicit teammate influences (i.e., bulimia modelling and teammate pressure) also have an increased role to play in disordered eating among Female athletes, it is noteworthy that this is not the case for male athletes where the severity of the critical comment was the only predictor of increased exercise psychopathology and drive for thinness. ";
  iadas:complexityOfAnalysis "Simple analyses";
  iadas:groupStatisticalAnalysis "Regression";
  iadas:hasPopulation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Population_1965>;
  iadas:hasRelation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Relations_1965>;
  iadas:hasSport <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Lean%20sport>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Non%20lean%20sport>;
  iadas:limites "The critical comment group had a higher prevalence of athletes with an eating disorder history. It is possible that the critical comment was antecedent to their eating disorder. Cross sectional design, effect relationships cannot be determined. The social Hassless Questionnaire asks athletes to reflect on the impact of a single, albeit, harmful, critical comment. It is therefore not possible to ascertain whether the impact on wellbeing was from this particular comment or perhaps more likely from frequent comments received over time. The self report restrospective nature of the SHQ meant that the accuracy of findings could be limited by poor recall/reconstruction (e.g., some athletes referred to comments made several years ago). ";
  iadas:numberOfSportStudied "Multiple";
  iadas:perspectives """Athletes and coaches could be mandated by their organisation to attend workshops highlighting how to combat negative body commentary (e.g., list of justifications for what is/is not appropriate communication) as well as equipping athletes with strategies to reduce body ideal internalisation (e.g., via media literacy training, enhancement of self compassion, promotion of body image flexibility). 
Future research should employ longitudinal designs with frequent sampling points which track athletes’ wellbeing in response to critical comments they receive from a variety of sources over time. Furthermore, future longitudinal research would benefit from exploring the impact of variables that may potentially mediate the relationship between critical comment severity and eating psychopathology. For example, poor psychological wellbeing has been found to exacerbate the relationship between negative teammate influences and eating outcomes, while self-compassion has been found to protect against the internalisation of weight stigma and social support seeking is a common coping strategy employed to reduce the negative impact of weight-related teasing. Finally, to improve specificity to male athlete populations, researchers should employ measures that assess muscularity enhancing behaviours that are also likely to be driven by critical comments. """;
  iadas:relationDegree " 𝛃=.20, p=.005";
  iadas:relationDirection "+";
  iadas:sampleSizeMobilized "127";
  iadas:sportPracticeType "Mixed sport";
  iadas:sportSubcategory "Multisport ";
  iadas:typeOfAnalysis "Predictive relationship/regression" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Analysis_1966> a iadas:Analysis;
  iadas:analysisId "1966";
  iadas:authorConclusion "Critical comment severity was found to be more important than implicit teammate influeces when explaining increased drive for thinness, body dissatisfaction for Female athletes. Implicit teammate influences (i.e., bulimia modelling and teammate pressure) also have an increased role to play in disordered eating among Female athletes, it is noteworthy that this is not the case for male athletes where the severity of the critical comment was the only predictor of increased exercise psychopathology and drive for thinness. ";
  iadas:complexityOfAnalysis "Simple analyses";
  iadas:groupStatisticalAnalysis "Regression";
  iadas:hasPopulation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Population_1966>;
  iadas:hasRelation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Relations_1966>;
  iadas:hasSport <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Lean%20sport>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Non%20lean%20sport>;
  iadas:limites "The critical comment group had a higher prevalence of athletes with an eating disorder history. It is possible that the critical comment was antecedent to their eating disorder. Cross sectional design, effect relationships cannot be determined. The social Hassless Questionnaire asks athletes to reflect on the impact of a single, albeit, harmful, critical comment. It is therefore not possible to ascertain whether the impact on wellbeing was from this particular comment or perhaps more likely from frequent comments received over time. The self report restrospective nature of the SHQ meant that the accuracy of findings could be limited by poor recall/reconstruction (e.g., some athletes referred to comments made several years ago). ";
  iadas:numberOfSportStudied "Multiple";
  iadas:perspectives """Athletes and coaches could be mandated by their organisation to attend workshops highlighting how to combat negative body commentary (e.g., list of justifications for what is/is not appropriate communication) as well as equipping athletes with strategies to reduce body ideal internalisation (e.g., via media literacy training, enhancement of self compassion, promotion of body image flexibility). 
Future research should employ longitudinal designs with frequent sampling points which track athletes’ wellbeing in response to critical comments they receive from a variety of sources over time. Furthermore, future longitudinal research would benefit from exploring the impact of variables that may potentially mediate the relationship between critical comment severity and eating psychopathology. For example, poor psychological wellbeing has been found to exacerbate the relationship between negative teammate influences and eating outcomes, while self-compassion has been found to protect against the internalisation of weight stigma and social support seeking is a common coping strategy employed to reduce the negative impact of weight-related teasing. Finally, to improve specificity to male athlete populations, researchers should employ measures that assess muscularity enhancing behaviours that are also likely to be driven by critical comments. """;
  iadas:relationDegree "𝛃=.09, p=.28";
  iadas:relationDirection "NS";
  iadas:sampleSizeMobilized "127";
  iadas:sportPracticeType "Mixed sport";
  iadas:sportSubcategory "Multisport ";
  iadas:typeOfAnalysis "Predictive relationship/regression" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Analysis_1967> a iadas:Analysis;
  iadas:analysisId "1967";
  iadas:authorConclusion "High level of anxiety mediated the relationships between teammates bulimic type behaviours and athlete engagement with drive for thinness behaviours and compulsive exercise to prevent negative affect and to control weight. Higher level of anxiety and depression were found to mediate the direct relationships between perceived pressure from teammates to lose weight or change shape and greater reported body dissatisfaction. Gender was not a significant moderator, meaning that the role of psychological wellbeing in the relationships between teammate influences and eating. ";
  iadas:complexityOfAnalysis "Simple analyses";
  iadas:groupStatisticalAnalysis "Correlation";
  iadas:hasPopulation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Population_1967>;
  iadas:hasRelation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Relations_1967>;
  iadas:hasSport <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Aesthetic>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/ball-game>,
    <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/endurance>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/power>,
    <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/team>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/technical>,
    <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/weight-class>;
  iadas:limites "Although age and BMI were controlled for, it is acknowledged that there are a number of additional co-variates (e.g., perfectionism, athletic identity) that could have contributed to the findings of this study. The self report nature of the data collected and the context it was collected in (i.e., before/after a training session and in the company of teammates/coach) may potentially misrepresent athletes true behaviours. Items comprising the EDI-drive for thinness and body dissatsifaction subscales may not be an accurate reflection of male athletes experiences with disordered eating. ";
  iadas:numberOfSportStudied "Multiple";
  iadas:perspectives "Identifying key risk factors and relevant moderators and mediators for eating psychopathology is important for effectively identifying those athletes at increased risk of eating and exercise psychopathology, and for developing appropriately tailored intervention and prevention strategies. Future research into the role of teammates would benefit from experimentally manipulating mechanisms of teammate influence on athletes actual food intake and should consider employing methodologies that are specifically tailored to male athletes. Employing modeeling analytic techniques to further our understanding of the complex, multi-factorial influences on athlete eating and exercise behaviours. ";
  iadas:relationDegree "r=.21, p<.01";
  iadas:relationDirection "+";
  iadas:sampleSizeMobilized "195";
  iadas:sportPracticeType "Mixed sport";
  iadas:sportSubcategory "Multisport ";
  iadas:typeOfAnalysis "Partial correlation" .

