<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Analysis_201> a iadas:Analysis;
  iadas:analysisId "201";
  iadas:authorConclusion "Body image has been found, not only to be a risk factor for the development of an ED, but as a mediator between perfectionism and dieting, and associated to performance cognitive anxiety which mediates the relationship between perfectionism and depression. ";
  iadas:complexityOfAnalysis "Simple analyses";
  iadas:groupStatisticalAnalysis "Correlation";
  iadas:hasPopulation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Population_201>;
  iadas:hasRelation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Relations_201>;
  iadas:hasSport <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/ballet>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/contemporary>,
    <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/flamenco>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/spanish%20folk>;
  iadas:limites "The use of general questionnaires such as the BDI II or EAT-26 which are not validated specifically to be used within the dance population, although they are validated and highly reliable measurements. Performance anxiety was measured hypothetically and it will be best to measure it previous to an event. Performance anxiety has been retrospectively measured instead of administering the questionnaires in situ before the performance. ";
  iadas:numberOfSportStudied "One";
  iadas:perspectives "Interventions aiming at reducing concern over mistakes may required a preventative component and by doing so it will help reducing body dissatisfaction, performance cognitive anxiety and dieting which will prevent the development of eating disorders in this vulnerable group. Future research should replicate this study and include males different age range, dance levels and nationalities. ";
  iadas:relationDegree "r=.47, p<.01";
  iadas:relationDirection "+";
  iadas:sampleSizeMobilized "281";
  iadas:sportPracticeType "Individual sport";
  iadas:sportSubcategory "Aesthetic";
  iadas:typeOfAnalysis "Pearson correlation " .

<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Analysis_2010> a iadas:Analysis;
  iadas:analysisId "2010";
  iadas:authorConclusion "Self critical perfectionism was found as the salient predictor of athletes eating psychopathology. Personal standards perfectionism was positively correlated with athletes eating psychopathology. Gender significantly moderate association between personal standards perfectionism and eating psychopathology, with the positive relationship found to be significant for Female athletes only. The findings revealed the relationship between self critical perfectionism and eating psychopathology to be of relevance only to Female athletes. ";
  iadas:complexityOfAnalysis "Simple analyses";
  iadas:groupStatisticalAnalysis "Correlation";
  iadas:hasPopulation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Population_2010>;
  iadas:hasRelation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Relations_2010>;
  iadas:hasSport <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Football>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/hockey>,
    <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/netball>;
  iadas:limites "Reliability of the CPQ among athletes, with the cronbach alpha value recording substantially bellow the recommaleded level. The use of self report measures. Cross sectional design, which has created difficulty in determining the temporal relations between perfectionism and eating psychopathology. The observed patterns cannot be generalised to the general population. ";
  iadas:numberOfSportStudied "Multiple";
  iadas:perspectives "Explore the similarities and differences in the relationship between perfectionism and eating psychopathology in a sample of clinical patients, non-athletes and athletes. ";
  iadas:relationDegree "r=.18, p<.01";
  iadas:relationDirection "+";
  iadas:sampleSizeMobilized "192";
  iadas:sportPracticeType "Team sport";
  iadas:sportSubcategory "Ball game";
  iadas:typeOfAnalysis "Spearman correlation" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Analysis_2011> a iadas:Analysis;
  iadas:analysisId "2011";
  iadas:authorConclusion "Self critical perfectionism was found as the salient predictor of athletes eating psychopathology. Personal standards perfectionism was positively correlated with athletes eating psychopathology. Gender significantly moderate association between personal standards perfectionism and eating psychopathology, with the positive relationship found to be significant for Female athletes only. The findings revealed the relationship between self critical perfectionism and eating psychopathology to be of relevance only to Female athletes. ";
  iadas:complexityOfAnalysis "Simple analyses";
