    <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/netball>;
  iadas:limites "Reliability of the CPQ among athletes, with the cronbach alpha value recording substantially bellow the recommaleded level. The use of self report measures. Cross sectional design, which has created difficulty in determining the temporal relations between perfectionism and eating psychopathology. The observed patterns cannot be generalised to the general population. ";
  iadas:numberOfSportStudied "Multiple";
  iadas:perspectives "Explore the similarities and differences in the relationship between perfectionism and eating psychopathology in a sample of clinical patients, non-athletes and athletes. ";
  iadas:relationDegree "r=.31, p<.001";
  iadas:relationDirection "+";
  iadas:sampleSizeMobilized "192";
  iadas:sportPracticeType "Team sport";
  iadas:sportSubcategory "Ball game";
  iadas:typeOfAnalysis "Spearman correlation" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Analysis_2013> a iadas:Analysis;
  iadas:analysisId "2013";
  iadas:authorConclusion "Avoidant attachmalet was associated with poor quality relationships with their influential parent and principal coach. Athletes who experiencd more conflict in their relationships were more likely to criticize themselves, which in turn can be related to low self esteem, with athletes feeling unvalued and worthless as indivdiuals. Subsequently, low self esteem was linked to higher depressive symptoms, which in turn was lined to eating psychopathology. Self critical perfectionism may play a more pivotal role in eating psychopathology than personal standard perfectionism. The possession of high self imposed standards may not be related to eating psychopathology. The psychopathological process of perfectionism and self esteem both seem to exert an indirect effect on eating psychopathology, while depression exerts a direct effect. ";
  iadas:complexityOfAnalysis "Simple analyses";
  iadas:groupStatisticalAnalysis "Correlation";
  iadas:hasPopulation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Population_2013>;
  iadas:hasRelation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Relations_2013>;
  iadas:hasSport <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Swimming>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/cycling>,
    <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/fencing>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/football>,
    <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/hockey>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/rugby>;
  iadas:limites "The study was cross sectional. There are some limitations related to the operational definitions and measurement of the constructs related to the transdiagnostic cognitive behavioral model. In this study only one aspect of the concept of mood intolerance was captured. The current study employed self report measures, results are subject to social desirability and acquiescence response bias. The study did not use a control group of non athletes and a sample of athletes with eating disorders. It therefore remains unclear whether the relationships observed would extend to the nonathletic population or to clinical populations. ";
  iadas:numberOfSportStudied "Multiple";
  iadas:perspectives "Continuing to develop a more encompassing understanding of the risk factors associated with eating disorders in athletes (e.g., examining psycho-social risk factors in addition to the sport-specific risk factors) wa may come a step closer in being able to prevent, manage and treat the disorder within the broader sporting community. ";
  iadas:relationDegree "r=.12, p<.01";
  iadas:relationDirection "+";
  iadas:sampleSizeMobilized "588";
  iadas:sportPracticeType "Mixed sport";
  iadas:sportSubcategory "Multisport ";
  iadas:typeOfAnalysis "Bivariate correlation" .

