<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Analysis_2198> a iadas:Analysis;
  iadas:analysisId "2198";
  iadas:authorConclusion "Higher body image (BI) was associated with lower muscle dysmorphia (MD), suggesting a protective role of positive body perception in weightlifters. While overall BI and MD did not vary by most demographics, BMI and age influenced specific MD symptoms, highlighting nuanced risk patterns";
  iadas:complexityOfAnalysis "Simple analyses";
  iadas:groupStatisticalAnalysis "Correlation";
  iadas:hasPopulation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Population_2198>;
  iadas:hasRelation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Relations_2198>;
  iadas:hasSport <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Weightlifting>;
  iadas:limites "The study’s cross-sectional design limits causal interpretation, and some findings (e.g., lack of gender differences) may be sample-specific. Additionally, focusing on total scores may overlook important variations within subdimensions";
  iadas:numberOfSportStudied "One";
  iadas:perspectives "Future research should explore these relationships longitudinally and examine psychosocial factors such as social media and training goals. Targeted interventions promoting positive body image could help reduce MD risk and improve athletes’ well-being";
  iadas:relationDegree "r=-.302, p<.001";
  iadas:relationDirection "-";
  iadas:sampleSizeMobilized "203";
  iadas:sportPracticeType "Individual sport";
  iadas:sportSubcategory "Power";
  iadas:typeOfAnalysis "Pearson correlation" .

