<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Analysis_221> a iadas:Analysis;
  iadas:analysisId "221";
  iadas:authorConclusion "No relationship between self-esteem, body image, and eating disorders risk. No significant correlations were found between the independent variables and eating disorder risk, and the independant variables did not predict eating disorder risk. ";
  iadas:complexityOfAnalysis "Simple analyses";
  iadas:groupStatisticalAnalysis "Correlation";
  iadas:hasPopulation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Population_221>;
  iadas:hasRelation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Relations_221>;
  iadas:hasSport <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Basketball>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Multiple%20sports>,
    <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Soccer>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Swimming>,
    <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Table%20Tennis>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Tae%20Kwon%20Do>,
    <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Volleyball>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/track%20and%20field>;
  iadas:limites """Awareness of the study could have biased the participant's reponses.
There was not an equal representation of gender since most of participants were males. 
The possibility of students being fearful exposing low self-esteem, negative body image, and/or eating disorder risk. 
Small sample size and low response rate, which was less than 30%. 
Lack of a control group due to time constraints and resources. """;
  iadas:numberOfSportStudied "Multiple";
  iadas:perspectives """Athletic departments should create preventive programs for the student-athletes. Schools should have health care professionals available for student-athletes to avoid future problems with eating disorders, self-esteem and body image. Also having a thorough physical and psychological examination of student-athletes before participation in sports is a great way to screen for possible needs. Athletic director and coaches can assist the student using a non-judgemntal approach to connect them with the most appropriate help. Athletics directors need to understand eating disorders, self-esteem, and body image. 
Academics could focus on conducted studies with other ethnic populations and use different variables in a much larger sample size. More psychosocial variables can be studied to analyze different correlations. Some of these variables might include gender, sports played, social media influence, coach's coaching style or method, and peer and parental influence, among others. A imilar representations of males and Females is also needed to analyze the differences between genders. Future research could focus on different parts of the Island, including the rural and uran areas. In addition, using a mixted methods intervention could provide more insight. Future research could conduct a more thorough analysis to obtain both quantitative and qualitative data.  In addition, it would e valuable to include a control group to compare student-athletes ith non-student-athletes. Finally a longitudinal study could be performed to examine eating disorder risk in student-athletes from high school to college. """;
  iadas:relationDegree "r=-.22, NS";
  iadas:relationDirection "NS";
  iadas:sampleSizeMobilized "36";
  iadas:sportPracticeType "Mixed sport";
  iadas:sportSubcategory "Multisport ";
  iadas:typeOfAnalysis "Pearson correlation" .

