<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Analysis_229> a iadas:Analysis;
  iadas:analysisId "229";
  iadas:authorConclusion "Goal orientation, body dissatisfaction, and negative behavior and pressure from coaches significantly contribute to the risk of ED development in female and male cadet, junior and non-elite athletes, while body dissatisfaction seems to be the strongest predictor, especially in female athletes. ";
  iadas:complexityOfAnalysis "Simple analyses";
  iadas:groupStatisticalAnalysis "Correlation";
  iadas:hasPopulation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Population_229>;
  iadas:hasRelation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Relations_229>;
  iadas:hasSport <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Taekwondo>;
  iadas:limites "The sample size, although it is realistic in relation to the number of active taekwondo competitors in Croatia. Self reported questionnaires. ";
  iadas:numberOfSportStudied "One";
  iadas:perspectives "Including other measures, more adjusted to male ED symptoms. As the results showed the significance of the behavior of coaches in relation to athletes ED symptoms, it would be very interesting to investigate the transgenerational transfer of distorted values, beliefs and behaviors related to ED from coaches to athletes, i.e., to investigate coaches history of ED symptoms and their current behavior. ";
  iadas:relationDegree "r=.01, N.S.";
  iadas:relationDirection "NS";
  iadas:sampleSizeMobilized "335";
  iadas:sportPracticeType "Individual sport";
  iadas:sportSubcategory "Weight sensitive";
  iadas:typeOfAnalysis "Spearman correlation" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Analysis_2291> a iadas:Analysis;
  iadas:analysisId "2291";
  iadas:authorConclusion "Eating attitudes and depressive symptoms were significantly associated. As for attitudes and behaviours related to physical appearance, only EAT scores suggestive of risk were significantly associated with giving up performing to avoid showing the body, feeling very affected comparing the body with others, believing that during performances the audience paid a lot of attention to their body and feeling very affected by it. ";
  iadas:complexityOfAnalysis "Simple analyses";
  iadas:groupStatisticalAnalysis "Correlation";
  iadas:hasPopulation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Population_2291>;
  iadas:hasRelation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Relations_2291>;
  iadas:hasSport <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Dance%20%28classical%20ballet%29>,
    <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Spanish%20dance>;
  iadas:limites "Our results may be biased by the lack of sincerity among informants or by a failure to report symptoms. The groups includes students of classical ballet and Spanish dance; these two disciplines are not excatly the same: classical ballet tends to underline thinness; this fact may have introduced a bias. We cannot obtain causal conclusions. Therefore we cannot state that particular pressures and school life events trigger risk eating behaviours. The lack of objective data supporting the existence of pressure from coaches. ";
  iadas:numberOfSportStudied "One";
  iadas:perspectives "Further studes should analyse whether these apparent differences in type of dance (classical ballet vs Spanish dance) represent different risks for ED. ";
  iadas:relationDegree "r=.31, p<.001";
  iadas:relationDirection "+";
  iadas:sampleSizeMobilized "105";
  iadas:sportPracticeType "Individual sport";
  iadas:sportSubcategory "Aesthetic";
  iadas:typeOfAnalysis "Correlation" .

