<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Analysis_23> a iadas:Analysis;
  iadas:analysisId "23";
  iadas:authorConclusion "As muscle dysmorphia increases, orthorexia nervosa increases in individuals engaged in bodybuilding sports in the TRNC. ";
  iadas:complexityOfAnalysis "Simple analyses";
  iadas:groupStatisticalAnalysis "Correlation";
  iadas:hasPopulation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Population_23>;
  iadas:hasRelation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Relations_23>;
  iadas:hasSport <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Bodybuilding>;
  iadas:limites "Since this study was conducted only on male bodybuilders, the findings cannot be generalized to Female bodybuilders.";
  iadas:numberOfSportStudied "One";
  iadas:perspectives """It is essential to provide education on muscle dysmorphia, orthorexia nervosa, and the use of ergogenic nutritional supplements in Physical Education and Sports Schools, as well as in coach training programs. Additionally, training on athlete nutrition and sports psychology in these institutions would equip them with knowledge about both athlete motivations and the mental health disorders frequently seen in athletes. Given the rise of these mental health issues in gyms, sports psychologists should be present in these environments, and mental health assessments should be conducted alongside medical evaluations for new memberships.
Conducting similar studies across different sports and with female participants could contribute valuable new insights to the literature.""";
  iadas:relationDegree "r=.48, p=.00";
  iadas:relationDirection "+";
  iadas:sampleSizeMobilized "200";
  iadas:sportPracticeType "Individual sport";
  iadas:sportSubcategory "Aesthetic";
  iadas:typeOfAnalysis "Pearson correlation " .

<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Analysis_230> a iadas:Analysis;
  iadas:analysisId "230";
  iadas:authorConclusion "Goal orientation, body dissatisfaction, and negative behavior and pressure from coaches significantly contribute to the risk of ED development in female and male cadet, junior and non-elite athletes, while body dissatisfaction seems to be the strongest predictor, especially in female athletes. ";
  iadas:complexityOfAnalysis "Simple analyses";
  iadas:groupStatisticalAnalysis "Correlation";
  iadas:hasPopulation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Population_230>;
  iadas:hasRelation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Relations_230>;
  iadas:hasSport <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Taekwondo>;
  iadas:limites "The sample size, although it is realistic in relation to the number of active taekwondo competitors in Croatia. Self reported questionnaires. ";
  iadas:numberOfSportStudied "One";
  iadas:perspectives "Including other measures, more adjusted to male ED symptoms. As the results showed the significance of the behavior of coaches in relation to athletes ED symptoms, it would be very interesting to investigate the transgenerational transfer of distorted values, beliefs and behaviors related to ED from coaches to athletes, i.e., to investigate coaches history of ED symptoms and their current behavior. ";
  iadas:relationDegree "r=.17, p<.01";
  iadas:relationDirection "+";
  iadas:sampleSizeMobilized "335";
  iadas:sportPracticeType "Individual sport";
  iadas:sportSubcategory "Weight sensitive";
  iadas:typeOfAnalysis "Spearman correlation" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Analysis_2300> a iadas:Analysis;
  iadas:analysisId "2300";
  iadas:authorConclusion "This study has extended prior work by investigating the role of exercise motives in the link between low body esteem and eating disorder symptomatology. ";
  iadas:complexityOfAnalysis "Simple analyses";
  iadas:groupStatisticalAnalysis "Correlation";
  iadas:hasPopulation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Population_2300>;
  iadas:hasRelation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Relations_2300>;
  iadas:hasSport <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Fitness>;
  iadas:limites "Cross sectional design does not allow any conclusions about the direction of causality of the variables. The small sample size. The procedure employed may have led to a selective sample. Results are limited in generalizability as they can be applied only to women who regularly exercise in a fitness center. ";
  iadas:numberOfSportStudied "One";
  iadas:perspectives "Future research is needed to examine current research topic in populations of exercisers other than fitness club members. Future research should replicate results in males, and people involved in other sports than aerobics. Future research should examine other reasons for exercise such as obsessivity-compulsivity. ";
  iadas:relationDegree "r=-.59, p<.01";
  iadas:relationDirection "-";
  iadas:sampleSizeMobilized "81";
  iadas:sportPracticeType "Individual sport";
  iadas:sportSubcategory "Aesthetic";
  iadas:typeOfAnalysis "Correlation" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Analysis_2303> a iadas:Analysis;
  iadas:analysisId "2303";
  iadas:authorConclusion "All sport related weight pressures were significantly related to disordered eating, however only self consciousness of weight and appearance emerged as a significant correlate in the regression model. Body dissatisfaction explained an additional 5.7% of the variance in disordered eating. Sport related body dissatisfaction also emerged as a significant predictor. Positive perfectionism made a significant contribution, but negative perfectionism did not. Self esteem may not be strongly related to disordered eating. Athletic identity did not emerge as a significant variable in the final regression model, although the bivariate correlation indicated a significant positive relationship with disordered eating. ";
  iadas:complexityOfAnalysis "Simple analyses";
  iadas:groupStatisticalAnalysis "Correlation";
  iadas:hasPopulation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Population_2303>;
  iadas:hasRelation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Relations_2303>;
  iadas:hasSport <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Figure%20skating%20%28ice%20dancing%29>,
    <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Figure%20skating%20%28multiple%29>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Figure%20skating%20%28pairs%29>,
    <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Figure%20skating%20%28singles%29>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Figure%20skating%20%28synchronized%29>;
  iadas:limites "Societal weight pressures were not exmained. Self report nature of the surveys and the willingness of athletes to honestly and accurately respond to survey items. The results are also based on a conveniance sample of skaters limited to clubs in the Midwest and Northeast. Cross sectional design, causal relationships and change over time could not be determined. ";
  iadas:numberOfSportStudied "One";
  iadas:perspectives """Sport psychology practiitioners should aid this effort by teaching skaters to appreciate body strengths and utilize psychological skills that may buffer the impact of negative weight pressures (e.g., utilizing positive self talk and realistic goal-setting). 
A random and more representative sample of skaters nationally and internationally, inclusive of males, should be considered in future research. """;
  iadas:relationDegree "r=.70, p<.01";
  iadas:relationDirection "+";
  iadas:sampleSizeMobilized "272";
  iadas:sportPracticeType "Individual sport";
  iadas:sportSubcategory "Aesthetic";
  iadas:typeOfAnalysis "Correlation" .

