<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Analysis_235> a iadas:Analysis;
  iadas:analysisId "235";
  iadas:authorConclusion "Goal orientation, body dissatisfaction, and negative behavior and pressure from coaches significantly contribute to the risk of ED development in female and male cadet, junior and non-elite athletes, while body dissatisfaction seems to be the strongest predictor, especially in female athletes. ";
  iadas:complexityOfAnalysis "Simple analyses";
  iadas:groupStatisticalAnalysis "Correlation";
  iadas:hasPopulation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Population_235>;
  iadas:hasRelation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Relations_235>;
  iadas:hasSport <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Taekwondo>;
  iadas:limites "The sample size, although it is realistic in relation to the number of active taekwondo competitors in Croatia. Self reported questionnaires. ";
  iadas:numberOfSportStudied "One";
  iadas:perspectives "Including other measures, more adjusted to male ED symptoms. As the results showed the significance of the behavior of coaches in relation to athletes ED symptoms, it would be very interesting to investigate the transgenerational transfer of distorted values, beliefs and behaviors related to ED from coaches to athletes, i.e., to investigate coaches history of ED symptoms and their current behavior. ";
  iadas:relationDegree "r=-.01, N.S.";
  iadas:relationDirection "NS";
  iadas:sampleSizeMobilized "132 (male)";
  iadas:sportPracticeType "Individual sport";
  iadas:sportSubcategory "Weight sensitive";
  iadas:typeOfAnalysis "Spearman correlation" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Analysis_2352> a iadas:Analysis;
  iadas:analysisId "2352";
  iadas:authorConclusion "Emotion regulation difficulty was positively associated with disorder eating pattern in our study. The non accept dimension was identified as a significant predictor of EAT-26. Emotion regulation did partially mediate the link between athletic status and disordered eating. ";
  iadas:complexityOfAnalysis "Simple analyses";
  iadas:groupStatisticalAnalysis "Correlation";
  iadas:hasPopulation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Population_2352>;
  iadas:hasRelation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Relations_2352>;
  iadas:hasSport <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Soccer>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/basketball>,
    <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/cheer>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/cross-country>,
    <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/dance>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/equestrian>,
    <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/golf>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/pom>,
    <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/softball>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/tennis>,
    <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/track%20and%20field>;
  iadas:limites "We utilized a convenience sample of Female college students ; no representative of the national population of Female college students as geographical, ethnic and other differences. Data were self reported and some participants may not have disclosed truthful information for different reasons (i.e., denial of eating disturbances, social bias). Athletes with high relative prevalence of DE were not represented in our sample because the university did not have such teams at the time of the study. ";
  iadas:numberOfSportStudied "Multiple";
  iadas:perspectives "Independent of family history of eating disorders and emotion regulation difficulties, characteristics of Female college athletes should be examined further to identify potential mediators through which athletic status serves as a protective measure from DE risks. Further investigate potential correlates and predictors of DE with large and representative samples of Female athletes that would allow for comparison by types of sport. ";
  iadas:relationDegree "r=.29, p<.001";
  iadas:relationDirection "+";
  iadas:sampleSizeMobilized "527";
  iadas:sportPracticeType "Mixed sport";
  iadas:sportSubcategory "Multisport ";
  iadas:typeOfAnalysis "Correlation" .

