<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Analysis_318> a iadas:Analysis;
  iadas:analysisId "318";
  iadas:authorConclusion "This longitudinal study contributes to understanding of the relationship between poor body image and eating psychopathology over time, and across sporting sub-samples (competitive, non-competitive and sports non-engagers). Poor body appreciation and better appearance-related sporting body image predicted eating psychopathology for the whole sample.";
  iadas:complexityOfAnalysis "Simple analyses";
  iadas:groupStatisticalAnalysis "Regression";
  iadas:hasPopulation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Population_318>;
  iadas:hasRelation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Relations_318>;
  iadas:hasSport <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Running>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/crossfit>,
    <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/cycling>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/dance>,
    <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/gymgoer%20HIIT>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/gymgoer%20weights>,
    <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/gymnastics>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/hiking>,
    <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/swimming>;
  iadas:limites "The longitudinal analysis were under-powered and could not be conducted separately for each group. The sample had a majority of females, meaning that any gender-specific regression analysis would have been underpowered for males. The age range was wider than in other studies and individuals engaged in a range of different sports, which might have impacted findings. The measures might not have been the optimum set. ";
  iadas:numberOfSportStudied "Multiple";
  iadas:perspectives "Futures studies could recruit different samples to explain the body image-eating psychopathology link, such as all genders and gender identities and other sports types. Ethnicity should also be considered, given the fact that the thin-ideal is differently experienced in different groups. ";
  iadas:relationDegree """𝛃=.79, N.S.
R=.79, N.S.
R²=.63, p<.001
R²change=.63
""";
  iadas:relationDirection "NS";
  iadas:sampleSizeMobilized "230";
  iadas:sportPracticeType "Mixed sport";
  iadas:sportSubcategory "Multisport ";
  iadas:typeOfAnalysis "Simple linear regression" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Analysis_32> a iadas:Analysis;
  iadas:analysisId "32";
  iadas:authorConclusion "As muscle dysmorphia increases, orthorexia nervosa increases in individuals engaged in bodybuilding sports in the TRNC. ";
  iadas:complexityOfAnalysis "Simple analyses";
  iadas:groupStatisticalAnalysis "Correlation";
  iadas:hasPopulation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Population_32>;
  iadas:hasRelation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Relations_32>;
  iadas:hasSport <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Bodybuilding>;
  iadas:limites "Since this study was conducted only on male bodybuilders, the findings cannot be generalized to Female bodybuilders.";
  iadas:numberOfSportStudied "One";
  iadas:perspectives """It is essential to provide education on muscle dysmorphia, orthorexia nervosa, and the use of ergogenic nutritional supplements in Physical Education and Sports Schools, as well as in coach training programs. Additionally, training on athlete nutrition and sports psychology in these institutions would equip them with knowledge about both athlete motivations and the mental health disorders frequently seen in athletes. Given the rise of these mental health issues in gyms, sports psychologists should be present in these environments, and mental health assessments should be conducted alongside medical evaluations for new memberships.
Conducting similar studies across different sports and with female participants could contribute valuable new insights to the literature.""";
  iadas:relationDegree "r=.14, p=.03";
  iadas:relationDirection "+";
  iadas:sampleSizeMobilized "200";
  iadas:sportPracticeType "Individual sport";
  iadas:sportSubcategory "Aesthetic";
  iadas:typeOfAnalysis "Pearson correlation " .

<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Analysis_320> a iadas:Analysis;
  iadas:analysisId "320";
  iadas:authorConclusion "Athletes are susceptible to negative mental-health outcomes, better support systems are warranted to protect them from the harmful effects of eating disorders, and to support those who may already be sttruggling. ";
  iadas:complexityOfAnalysis "Simple analyses";
  iadas:groupStatisticalAnalysis "Correlation";
  iadas:hasPopulation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Population_320>;
  iadas:hasRelation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Relations_320>;
  iadas:hasSport <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Swimming>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/badminton>,
    <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/basketball>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/cross%20country>,
    <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/dance>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/field%20hockey>,
    <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/football>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/frisbee>,
    <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/lacrosse>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/rugby>,
    <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/soccer>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/volleyball>;
  iadas:limites "Athletes were aware of the nature of this research as they were informed about what we were studying prior to participation. Participation was voluntary and this may provoked individuals with personal experience with eating disorder behaviours to participate in the research, and discouraged those who were uninterested in the topic from completing the survey. This could change the perceptage and number of participant's concerning results. ";
  iadas:numberOfSportStudied "Multiple";
  iadas:perspectives "Receive professional education centred around other areas (including sexual violence and substance use), may benefit from increased training around eating behaviours, body image, and mental health. Universities can inregrate student resources within athletics to ensure that athletics are a positive activity for those who participate. ";
  iadas:relationDegree "r=.694, p<.001";
