<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Analysis_33> a iadas:Analysis;
  iadas:analysisId "33";
  iadas:authorConclusion "As muscle dysmorphia increases, orthorexia nervosa increases in individuals engaged in bodybuilding sports in the TRNC. ";
  iadas:complexityOfAnalysis "Simple analyses";
  iadas:groupStatisticalAnalysis "Correlation";
  iadas:hasPopulation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Population_33>;
  iadas:hasRelation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Relations_33>;
  iadas:hasSport <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Bodybuilding>;
  iadas:limites "Since this study was conducted only on male bodybuilders, the findings cannot be generalized to Female bodybuilders.";
  iadas:numberOfSportStudied "One";
  iadas:perspectives """It is essential to provide education on muscle dysmorphia, orthorexia nervosa, and the use of ergogenic nutritional supplements in Physical Education and Sports Schools, as well as in coach training programs. Additionally, training on athlete nutrition and sports psychology in these institutions would equip them with knowledge about both athlete motivations and the mental health disorders frequently seen in athletes. Given the rise of these mental health issues in gyms, sports psychologists should be present in these environments, and mental health assessments should be conducted alongside medical evaluations for new memberships.
Conducting similar studies across different sports and with female participants could contribute valuable new insights to the literature.""";
  iadas:relationDegree "r=.19, p=.005";
  iadas:relationDirection "+";
  iadas:sampleSizeMobilized "200";
  iadas:sportPracticeType "Individual sport";
  iadas:sportSubcategory "Aesthetic";
  iadas:typeOfAnalysis "Pearson correlation " .

<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Analysis_330> a iadas:Analysis;
  iadas:analysisId "330";
  iadas:authorConclusion "Self esteem is significaltly related to muscle dysmorphia. Perfectionism is non-significantly related to MD. Perfectionism socially prescribed is significantly related to MD among bodybuilders. Social phobia is significantly related to MD. Orthorexia is significantly related to MD among bodybuilders. Depression is significantly related to MD. ";
  iadas:complexityOfAnalysis "Simple analyses";
  iadas:groupStatisticalAnalysis "Correlation";
  iadas:hasPopulation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Population_330>;
  iadas:hasRelation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Relations_330>;
  iadas:hasSport <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Bodybuilding>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/fitness>,
    <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/strength%20training>;
  iadas:limites "First the sample size was relativelly small which likely reduces the generalizability of our conclusions. Related to this issue, the statisticcal power of the employed regression design could not guarantee a reliable detection of MD predictors. Secondly, the online recruitmalet strategy we adopted under-sampled those without internet capability and prevented the opportunity of conducting face-to-face interviews of participants self-identified as recreational bodybuilders and recreational strength athletess. Lastly, the absence of psychometric information for the MPS is another limitation. ";
  iadas:numberOfSportStudied "Multiple";
  iadas:perspectives "Improving the identification of predictors and risk factors for MD, as well as its associated psychological features, might facilitate the identification of at-risk trainers, as well as the development of prevention strategies and interventions. Furthermore, a deeper understanding of MD manifestation among athletes may help to distinguishing individuals participating in a healthy manner versus those participating in an unhealthy manner. ";
  iadas:relationDegree "r=-.45, p<.01";
  iadas:relationDirection "-";
  iadas:sampleSizeMobilized "42";
  iadas:sportPracticeType "Individual sport";
  iadas:sportSubcategory "Multisport ";
  iadas:typeOfAnalysis "Pearson correlation" .

