<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Analysis_356> a iadas:Analysis;
  iadas:analysisId "356";
  iadas:authorConclusion "The study show that autonomous motivation for sport had a significant, indirect and positive association with drive for muscularity via perceived behavioral control and intention to gain muscle, and that contolled motivation for sports was related to drive for muscularity both directly and via attitude and intention to gain muscle mass. These results indicate that the trans contextual model was partially supported in the context of bodybuiling, suggesting a complex motivational model underlying drive for muscularity in male bodybuilders. ";
  iadas:complexityOfAnalysis "Simple analyses";
  iadas:groupStatisticalAnalysis "Correlation";
  iadas:hasPopulation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Population_356>;
  iadas:hasRelation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Relations_356>;
  iadas:hasSport <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Bodybuilding>;
  iadas:limites "Our data are limited because our sample was composed only of young male with different levels of expertise (i.e., non-competitors, future competitors, competitors), which might have influenced their motivation and engagement in drive for muscularity. Second, subjective norm is a questionable variable as it might indicate a measurement issue in the sense that such variables reflect greater pressure and controlling rather than supportive forms of motivation. Third, the date were collected using a self-report survey and might have been influenced by social desirability. Fourth, the internal consistency of some measures was quite low and this might have been linked to our heterogeneous sample or to the psychometric quality concerning the controlled motivation scale, or the inconsistency between the external regulation and the introjected regulation. ";
  iadas:numberOfSportStudied "One";
  iadas:perspectives """It would be interesting to conduct a longitudinal study to more closely examine the causal relationship between variables. Methodologically, an important perspective for the future would be to determine a cutoff for drive for muscularity, which might then function as a reference when using drive for muscularity as a screening instrument for disordered eating. Moreover, eating disorders are very common on male bodybuilders and it might be of value to examine the relationships between motivation for sport, drive for muscularity and other specified feeding or eating disorders such as food restriction or night eating syndrome, or binge eating. 
Preventive actions may be important for male bodybuilders, who focus on gaining muscle mass. Specifically, such actions should be directed toward helping them to avoid developing controlled motivation because, although motivation for sport of any kind can be a direct or indirect risk factor for the development of deviant behavior, controlled motivation seems to put them at greater risk. It may therefore be important to carefully keep track of the development of motivation for sport to be sure that controlled motivation stays low, with moderate and healthy practice of bodybuilding. Moreover, the motivational mechanisms explaining the development of drive for muscularity could be deepened by complementary analysis in terms of motivational profiles. """;
  iadas:relationDegree "r=.21, p<.01";
  iadas:relationDirection "+";
  iadas:sampleSizeMobilized "171";
  iadas:sportPracticeType "Individual sport";
  iadas:sportSubcategory "Aesthetic";
  iadas:typeOfAnalysis "Pearson correlation" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Analysis_357> iadas:analysisId "357";
  iadas:authorConclusion "The study show that autonomous motivation for sport had a significant, indirect and positive association with drive for muscularity via perceived behavioral control and intention to gain muscle, and that contolled motivation for sports was related to drive for muscularity both directly and via attitude and intention to gain muscle mass. These results indicate that the trans contextual model was partially supported in the context of bodybuiling, suggesting a complex motivational model underlying drive for muscularity in male bodybuilders. ";
  iadas:complexityOfAnalysis "Simple analyses";
  iadas:groupStatisticalAnalysis "Correlation";
  iadas:hasPopulation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Population_357>;
  iadas:hasRelation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Relations_357>;
  iadas:hasSport <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Bodybuilding>;
  iadas:limites "Our data are limited because our sample was composed only of young male with different levels of expertise (i.e., non-competitors, future competitors, competitors), which might have influenced their motivation and engagement in drive for muscularity. Second, subjective norm is a questionable variable as it might indicate a measurement issue in the sense that such variables reflect greater pressure and controlling rather than supportive forms of motivation. Third, the date were collected using a self-report survey and might have been influenced by social desirability. Fourth, the internal consistency of some measures was quite low and this might have been linked to our heterogeneous sample or to the psychometric quality concerning the controlled motivation scale, or the inconsistency between the external regulation and the introjected regulation. ";
  iadas:numberOfSportStudied "One";
  iadas:perspectives """It would be interesting to conduct a longitudinal study to more closely examine the causal relationship between variables. Methodologically, an important perspective for the future would be to determine a cutoff for drive for muscularity, which might then function as a reference when using drive for muscularity as a screening instrument for disordered eating. Moreover, eating disorders are very common on male bodybuilders and it might be of value to examine the relationships between motivation for sport, drive for muscularity and other specified feeding or eating disorders such as food restriction or night eating syndrome, or binge eating. 
Preventive actions may be important for male bodybuilders, who focus on gaining muscle mass. Specifically, such actions should be directed toward helping them to avoid developing controlled motivation because, although motivation for sport of any kind can be a direct or indirect risk factor for the development of deviant behavior, controlled motivation seems to put them at greater risk. It may therefore be important to carefully keep track of the development of motivation for sport to be sure that controlled motivation stays low, with moderate and healthy practice of bodybuilding. Moreover, the motivational mechanisms explaining the development of drive for muscularity could be deepened by complementary analysis in terms of motivational profiles. """;
  iadas:relationDegree "r=.34, p<.01";
  iadas:relationDirection "+";
  iadas:sampleSizeMobilized "171";
  a iadas:Analysis;
  iadas:sportPracticeType "Individual sport";
  iadas:sportSubcategory "Aesthetic";
  iadas:typeOfAnalysis "Pearson correlation" .

