<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Analysis_5> a iadas:Analysis;
  iadas:analysisId "5";
  iadas:authorConclusion "This study supports a dual-pathway model of muscle dysmorphia, where sociocultural pressure influences symptoms both indirectly via appearance motivation and directly, with cognitive flexibility acting as a protective moderator. It highlights the interplay between social, motivational, and cognitive factors";
  iadas:complexityOfAnalysis "Simple analyses";
  iadas:groupStatisticalAnalysis "Correlation";
  iadas:hasPopulation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Population_5>;
  iadas:hasRelation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Relations_5>;
  iadas:limites "The cross-sectional design limits causal interpretation, and the use of a convenience, all-male sample restricts generalizability. Additionally, the measure of sociocultural pressure was not specific to muscularity ideals";
  iadas:numberOfSportStudied "Multiple";
  iadas:perspectives "Future research should use longitudinal designs and more diverse samples to validate the model. Interventions enhancing cognitive flexibility and promoting non-appearance-based exercise motives appear promising avenues for prevention and treatment.";
  iadas:relationDegree "r=-.305, p<.01";
  iadas:relationDirection "-";
  iadas:sampleSizeMobilized "95";
  iadas:sportPracticeType "Mixed sport";
  iadas:sportSubcategory "Multisport";
  iadas:typeOfAnalysis "Pearson correlation" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Analysis_50> a iadas:Analysis;
  iadas:analysisId "50";
  iadas:authorConclusion "Protective and facilitative role of self-compassion in body appreciation, intuitive eating, disordered eating, compulsive exercise, and self-criticism for Canadian Female athletes";
  iadas:complexityOfAnalysis "Simple analyses";
  iadas:groupStatisticalAnalysis "Correlation";
  iadas:hasPopulation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Population_50>;
  iadas:hasRelation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Relations_50>;
  iadas:hasSport <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/cheerleading>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/dance>,
    <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/track%20and%20field>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/volleyball>;
  iadas:limites "An updated version of the BAS was published after all study data had been collected.";
  iadas:numberOfSportStudied "Multiple";
  iadas:perspectives """(a) Explore Female athletes’ self-compassion, body and eating attitudes and behaviors overtime and within specific sport and international cultural contexts.
(b) it is unclear how body appreciation and intuitive eating are related to sport performance.""";
  iadas:relationDegree "r=.94, p<.01";
  iadas:relationDirection "+";
  iadas:sampleSizeMobilized "90";
  iadas:sportPracticeType "Mixed sport";
  iadas:sportSubcategory "Multisport ";
  iadas:typeOfAnalysis "Pearson correlation " .

<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Analysis_500> a iadas:Analysis;
  iadas:analysisId "500";
  iadas:authorConclusion "There is a significant relationship between parental psychological control and both exercise dependence and eating disorder symptoms. Psychological control, both maternal and parternal, predicted maladaptive perfectionism, while maladaptive perfectionism, predicted ED symptoms and exercise dependence symptoms. Results confirmed the mediating role of maladaptive perfectionism as a full mediator for ED and exercise dependence symptoms for both male and Female habitual exercisers in the maternal data. In the paternal data, maladaptive perfectionism mediated the relationships between paternal psychological control and eating disorder and exercise dependence symptoms as full mediator for the Female participants and as partial mediator for the male participants. ";
  iadas:complexityOfAnalysis "Simple analyses";
  iadas:groupStatisticalAnalysis "Correlation";
  iadas:hasPopulation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Population_500>;
  iadas:hasRelation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Relations_500>;
  iadas:limites "First, the data were collected at one time point and not across multiple time points as it should be done for a true mediational analysis. Because of the cross-sectional and self-report design of our study, we cannot etablish either the direction or causality of the effects. As weel, the self-report data may have intrinsic limitations such as social desirability, so the use of objective and multi-informant measures and experimental designs are encourages. Although, our sample consisted of habitual exercisers, this sample was deemed justified, as eating disorder and exercise dependence symptoms are prevalent in this population and because psychological control is a risk factor for eating disorder and exercise dependence symptoms in this population. ";
  iadas:numberOfSportStudied "Multiple";
  iadas:perspectives "The deleterious effects of parental psychological control, both on eating disorder and on exercise dependence symptoms, require the implementation of targeted interventions to educate parents in using fewer instrusions and parental autonomy support, as opposed to psychological control. It is important to educate parents about psychological control being a universally negative parenting strategy and to help parents identify and reduce the use of such dysfunctional practices. Parent training could be used to help parents to get to practice autonomy supportive skills by taking part in various exercices from common situations in familial daily living. Results of the study suggest that the therapist and client could work together to identify and correct the client's maladaptive perfectionism thought processes that result in maladaptive eating or exercise behavior. ";
  iadas:relationDegree " r=.27, p<.01";
  iadas:relationDirection "+";
  iadas:sampleSizeMobilized "170";
  iadas:sportPracticeType "Mixed sport";
  iadas:sportSubcategory "Multisport ";
  iadas:typeOfAnalysis "Correlation" .

