  iadas:authorConclusion "This study identifies elite karate athletes as a high-risk group for disordered eating and orthorexia, with body image—especially in women—and weight-related pressures playing a central role. It highlights the importance of psychological factors over BMI in shaping risk.";
  iadas:complexityOfAnalysis "Simple analyses";
  iadas:groupStatisticalAnalysis "Correlation";
  iadas:hasPopulation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Population_588>;
  iadas:hasRelation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Relations_588>;
  iadas:hasSport <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Karate>;
  iadas:limites "The cross-sectional design, small and selective sample, and reliance on self-reported data limit causal inference and generalizability. The use of BMI and omission of key psychosocial variables further constrain interpretation.";
  iadas:numberOfSportStudied "One";
  iadas:perspectives "Future research should include larger samples, longitudinal designs, and objective body composition measures. Preventive interventions focusing on body image, nutrition education, and sport-specific pressures are essential in weight-category sport";
  iadas:relationDegree "r=-.547, p<.001";
  iadas:relationDirection "-";
  iadas:sampleSizeMobilized "33";
  iadas:sportPracticeType "Individual sport";
  iadas:sportSubcategory "Weight sensitive";
  iadas:typeOfAnalysis "Pearson correlation" .

