<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Analysis_719> a iadas:Analysis;
  iadas:analysisId "719";
  iadas:authorConclusion "Actual weight and low self esteem were not predictive of DE; no significant relationship was found between body esteem for appearance and eating attitudes scores. Only body esteem for weight and body esteem attribution emerged as statistically significant. There were correlations between self-esteem and body self-esteem in the three groups, components of body esteem appear to be better predictors of EAT scores. ";
  iadas:complexityOfAnalysis "Simple analyses";
  iadas:groupStatisticalAnalysis "Correlation";
  iadas:hasPopulation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Population_719>;
  iadas:hasRelation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Relations_719>;
  iadas:hasSport <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Synchronized%20swimming>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/rhythmic%20gymnastics>;
  iadas:limites "First the sample involved 152 Female adolescents. Their consent was obtained after an informational session regarding the study, so some Female with eating issues may have chosen not to participate. Second, the relatively small number of participants in each group can suggest the caution should be applied in generalizing results. It should be noted that the problem of an important heteroscedasticity of the EAT-26 scores which cannot be solved by data transformation dissuaded us from using a global model. Third, it should be noted that the data collected for this study were self-reported. The self-report method can result in under-reporting of disordered eating symptoms in Female athletes. ";
  iadas:numberOfSportStudied "Multiple";
  iadas:perspectives "Further research should involve a larger sample of aesthetic athletes and provide access to a wider variety of aesthetic sports. However, it would also be interesting to differentiate the role of direct and indirect social pressure in aesthetic sports and to understand how Female athletes interpret and react differently to them to achieve the perceived ideal body image. It would be interesting not to focus just on the pressures exerced by the coach but also to question the influence of peers. A clearer understanding og how these different pressures operate and interact may provide crucial information for the perceptions of the role of others and guide the content of specific intervention programmes. ";
  iadas:relationDegree "r=.147, N.S.";
  iadas:relationDirection "NS";
  iadas:sampleSizeMobilized "61";
  iadas:sportPracticeType "Individual sport";
  iadas:sportSubcategory "Aesthetic";
  iadas:typeOfAnalysis "Correlation" .

