<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Analysis_757> a iadas:Analysis;
  iadas:analysisId "757";
  iadas:authorConclusion "The predictors of disordered eating for Female were BMI, age, drive for muscularity and the general internalization of socially etablished ideals, and for male it was the muscularity-oriented behaviors. ";
  iadas:complexityOfAnalysis "Simple analyses";
  iadas:groupStatisticalAnalysis "Correlation";
  iadas:hasPopulation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Population_757>;
  iadas:hasRelation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Relations_757>;
  iadas:hasSport <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Crossfit>;
  iadas:limites "First, the sample may not be representative of the Brazilian population, as it was carried out in a single city in a single state. Second, there is a need to study other constructs, such as body dissatisfaction, which can predict other aspects of disordered eating in this population. Third, reported weight en height may be a bias, depiste being a common finding in body image  and eating behavior studies? Finally, even though the EAT-26 is frequently used in Brazil, the measure is not validated for adults in the country. Moreover, the EAT-26 appears to be a more focused instrument for investigating the symptoms of disordered eating often seen in Female, while recent studies have shown that disordered eating among male has its own characteristics.";
  iadas:numberOfSportStudied "One";
  iadas:perspectives "Performance is directly related to diet, some disordered nutritional strategies can impair this performance and increase the risk of eating disorders in athletes. ";
  iadas:relationDegree "r=.43, p<.001";
  iadas:relationDirection "+";
  iadas:sampleSizeMobilized "91";
  iadas:sportPracticeType "Individual sport";
  iadas:sportSubcategory "Power";
  iadas:typeOfAnalysis "Correlation" .

