  iadas:limites "First, the sample may not be representative of the Brazilian population, as it was carried out in a single city in a single state. Second, there is a need to study other constructs, such as body dissatisfaction, which can predict other aspects of disordered eating in this population. Third, reported weight en height may be a bias, depiste being a common finding in body image  and eating behavior studies? Finally, even though the EAT-26 is frequently used in Brazil, the measure is not validated for adults in the country. Moreover, the EAT-26 appears to be a more focused instrument for investigating the symptoms of disordered eating often seen in Female, while recent studies have shown that disordered eating among male has its own characteristics.";
  iadas:numberOfSportStudied "One";
  iadas:perspectives "Performance is directly related to diet, some disordered nutritional strategies can impair this performance and increase the risk of eating disorders in athletes. ";
  iadas:relationDegree "r=.13, ns ";
  iadas:relationDirection "NS";
  iadas:sampleSizeMobilized "91";
  iadas:sportPracticeType "Individual sport";
  iadas:sportSubcategory "Power";
  iadas:typeOfAnalysis "Correlation" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Analysis_824> a iadas:Analysis;
  iadas:analysisId "824";
  iadas:authorConclusion "There is a positive association of body dissatisfaction with risk behaviour for eating disorders and addiction to exercise. ";
  iadas:complexityOfAnalysis "Simple analyses";
  iadas:groupStatisticalAnalysis "Correlation";
  iadas:hasPopulation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Population_824>;
  iadas:hasRelation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Relations_824>;
  iadas:hasSport <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Fitness>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/crossfit>;
  iadas:limites """Small number of participants and who were practitioners of only two modalities, which males impossible to generalize th results to practitioners of other exercises. 
The cross sectional design which does not allow to make inferences of causality between the variables. """;
  iadas:numberOfSportStudied "Multiple";
  iadas:perspectives """Future researches should extend the study with practitioners from other regions of Brazilian population and exercises. 
A longitudinal study will be able to point to the causal nature of the association between BD, addiction to exercise and risk behavior for ED. 
It is important that instructors and personal trainers analyse the real motives of the individuals to begin the practice of the exercise, always considering the chosen modality. Moreover, it is essential to emphasize the effects of long term training focusing on maintaining health and quality of life, always respecting the individuality and healthy evolution of training. """;
  iadas:relationDegree "r=-.42, p<.05";
  iadas:relationDirection "-";
  iadas:sampleSizeMobilized "60";
  iadas:sportPracticeType "Individual sport";
  iadas:sportSubcategory "Multisport ";
  iadas:typeOfAnalysis "Correlation" .

