<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Analysis_882> a iadas:Analysis;
  iadas:analysisId "882";
  iadas:authorConclusion "The severity of appearance-based critical comments, and lower self esteem were associated with heightened eating psychopathology. Critical comments could negatively impact on the dancer's psychological well-being, such as lowering self-esteem, which in turn could increase the risk for eating psychopathology. ";
  iadas:complexityOfAnalysis "Simple analyses";
  iadas:groupStatisticalAnalysis "Regression";
  iadas:hasPopulation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Population_882>;
  iadas:hasRelation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Relations_882>;
  iadas:hasSport <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Dance>;
  iadas:limites "The use of questionnaires, which may underestimate the levels of eating, weight or shape concerns. The cross-sectional design meant that it is impossible to ascertain whther those in the critical comments group had already had heightened eating, weight and shape concerns, which led thme to interprating body weight and shape comments from a teacher as being critical; rather tha the comment itself leading to increased eating psychopatholoy. ";
  iadas:numberOfSportStudied "One";
  iadas:perspectives "Longitudinal study design. Future research should look to further examine the negative effect of critical comments on dancer's psychological well-being. ";
  iadas:relationDegree """𝛃=.69, p<.001
R²=.46""";
  iadas:relationDirection "+";
  iadas:sampleSizeMobilized "77";
  iadas:sportPracticeType "Individual sport";
  iadas:sportSubcategory "Aesthetic";
  iadas:typeOfAnalysis "Simple linear regression" .

<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Analysis_884> a iadas:Analysis;
  iadas:analysisId "884";
  iadas:authorConclusion "Regression analyses indicated that increased identity with exercise contributed to decreased eating pathology amongst half marathon runners. There was a significant interaction effect noted between exercise identity and compulsive exercise, such that the relation of exercise identity and eating disorders pathology was significant amongst those runners reporting either lower or higher compulsive exercise scores. The significant associations identified for half marathoners were not demonstrated in marathon runners. ";
  iadas:complexityOfAnalysis "Simple analyses";
  iadas:groupStatisticalAnalysis "Correlation";
  iadas:hasPopulation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Population_884>;
  iadas:hasRelation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Relations_884>;
  iadas:hasSport <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/Half%20marathon>, <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/sport-vocab/marathon>;
  iadas:limites "It is possible that individuals registered for the half marathon were of a more elite running status. This study was also dependent on cross-sectional, self reported data collection methods. The runners assessed were all adults who were registered for competitive events in the northeastern United States, and results may not necessarily generalize to other age groups, or geographical areas, including internationally.";
  iadas:numberOfSportStudied "Multiple";
  iadas:perspectives """Future work should investigate the relation between the study variables over time, allowing for investigation of the impact of athlete injury. Investigate variables amongst runners who are adolescent and young adult. 
Clinicians should increase screening for eating disorders and compulsive exercise across runners of less elite status, and integrate eating disorders prevention efforts amongst athletes who report low exercise identity and either lower or higher compulsive exercise. """;
  iadas:relationDegree " r=.02, N.S.";
  iadas:relationDirection "NS";
  iadas:sampleSizeMobilized "110";
  iadas:sportPracticeType "Individual sport";
  iadas:sportSubcategory "Endurance";
  iadas:typeOfAnalysis "Bivariate correlation" .

