<http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Analysis_9> a iadas:Analysis;
  iadas:analysisId "9";
  iadas:authorConclusion "This study supports a dual-pathway model of muscle dysmorphia, where sociocultural pressure influences symptoms both indirectly via appearance motivation and directly, with cognitive flexibility acting as a protective moderator. It highlights the interplay between social, motivational, and cognitive factors";
  iadas:complexityOfAnalysis "Simple analyses";
  iadas:groupStatisticalAnalysis "Correlation";
  iadas:hasPopulation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Population_9>;
  iadas:hasRelation <http://ns.inria.fr/iadas/data/Relations_9>;
  iadas:limites "The cross-sectional design limits causal interpretation, and the use of a convenience, all-male sample restricts generalizability. Additionally, the measure of sociocultural pressure was not specific to muscularity ideals";
  iadas:numberOfSportStudied "Multiple";
  iadas:perspectives "Future research should use longitudinal designs and more diverse samples to validate the model. Interventions enhancing cognitive flexibility and promoting non-appearance-based exercise motives appear promising avenues for prevention and treatment.";
  iadas:relationDegree "r=.427, p<.001";
  iadas:relationDirection "+";
  iadas:sampleSizeMobilized "95";
  iadas:sportPracticeType "Mixed sport";
  iadas:sportSubcategory "Multisport";
  iadas:typeOfAnalysis "Pearson correlation" .

